-- iqm_minimap -- F3: the route as a trail of marks on the HUD minimap.
--
-- A SECOND VIEW of the route iqm_nav already publishes, not a second route. Everything
-- about WHICH points exist, how far ahead they run and how visible each one is was decided
-- upstream; this module transforms them into minimap space and puts sprites there. No
-- pathing, no occlusion, no changes to the AR renderer.
--
--   iqm_nav.route_draw()  -->  iqm_core  (AR stroke + ground chevrons)
--                         -->  iqm_minimap  (this: marks on the HUD minimap)
--
-- The whole design and the evidence behind it is docs/minimap-route.md; section numbers
-- below point there. The four things that file settled, and that this code depends on:
--
--   * Draw order (section 2). A window added with AddDialogToRender lands in
--     DoRenderDialogs, which CUIGameCustom::Render runs AFTER UIMainIngameWnd -- and the
--     minimap lives in UIMainIngameWnd. So our marks land ON the map with no hook.
--     Confirmed on screen, F3d.
--   * The transform (section 3) is a plain 2D similarity: centre on the actor, rotate by
--     the camera heading, scale by pixels-per-metre. No perspective, so none of the
--     machinery the AR route needed (R2.13-R2.22 in docs/ar-navigation.md).
--   * The map is HEADING-UP and a spot's rect is reported AFTER the map's own rotation
--     (section 8.1, measured: rot = -camera to within 0.02 deg). So the rotation term is
--     free -- it comes from device().cam_dir and needs no reference object at all.
--   * Scale and centre must be MEASURED, not computed (section 4). Five mods ship a
--     minimap layout, the XML is chosen by aspect ratio so it has a different winner per
--     display, and CUIZoneMap::Init then rescales it by display options. Anything derived
--     from config is a guess about someone else's load order and monitor.
--
-- HOW CALIBRATION WORKS, and why it needs no spot of its own.
--
-- Since section 8.1 removed rotation from the unknowns, only three are left: the map
-- centre (cx, cy) and the scale (ppm). A single spot whose object position we know gives
-- two equations per observation, so TWO observations with sufficiently different geometry
-- determine all three -- and the actor supplies that difference for free simply by moving
-- and turning.
--
-- The spot we observe is the route target's own task marker. That is the one object
-- guaranteed to be both on the minimap and known to us exactly when the route is being
-- drawn, which is precisely when this module needs the transform. Nothing is spawned and
-- no spot type is added; if the target carries no minimap spot, calibration simply waits.
--
-- Deliberately NOT the alternative from section 4 -- our own transparent spot on the actor
-- -- because whether a fully transparent spot still yields a CUIStatic is untested (the
-- open half of F3a), and this route avoids needing to know.

local floor, sqrt, atan2 = math.floor, math.sqrt, math.atan2
local cos, sin           = math.cos, math.sin

-- The virtual UI space every static is placed in, and the space GetAbsoluteRect reports.
local UI_W, UI_H = 1024, 768

-- Aspect-correction the engine applies to rotated (heading) statics: it scales a rotated
-- element's X by this, so a heading computed from UI-space deltas has to be pre-divided by
-- it. Same value and the same reason as iqm_core' UI_KX; recomputed only when the
-- screen width changes.
local UI_KX = 1
local _ui_w = 0

-- Our own spot type, carried by the ACTOR for as long as the trail is switched on.
--
-- Declared by modxml_n_iqm_map_icons.script splicing gamedata/configs/ui/iqm_map_spots.xml
-- into map_spots*.xml, as a 1x1 minimap-only spot -- see that file for why one pixel rather
-- than a transparent texture.
--
-- It used to be placed on whatever object was handy and removed the moment a solve landed.
-- That was a consequence of borrowing OTHER objects' spots as references, which turned out
-- to be the wrong design entirely (see actor_read): the actor is the only object guaranteed
-- to be on the map, and its spot is worth keeping, since it is also the live map centre.
local CALIB = "iqm_calib"

-- Marks. iqm_nav caps its own chevron count, so this is a ceiling rather than a second
-- opinion about spacing: at the tightest gap the menu offers, a full route is ~26.
local MAX_MARKS = 26

-- Margin between the last mark and the map's edge, as a multiple of the mark's own size.
--
-- This used to be a flat 0.86 of the radius, which is the wrong SHAPE of correction: the
-- reason to pull in from the rim is that a mark is a sprite with width and sitting it
-- exactly on the boundary reads as clipped -- a reason that scales with the MARK, not with
-- the map. On a 40 m radius the old fraction threw away 5.6 m of perfectly good trail and
-- left a visible gap between the last mark and the edge.
--
-- 0.75 of the mark size is half the sprite plus a little air.
local EDGE_MARKS = 0.75

-- Metres across the minimap. CUIZoneMap sizes the map window to the level's bound_rect
-- times minimap_width/100, so the span is 100 m wherever minimap_zoom is unset -- which is
-- everywhere in this install's loose config (section 3). Kept as a constant rather than an
-- option because it is an engine fact, not a preference; `IQM: Minimap State` prints the
-- radius it implies so a layout that disagrees is visible rather than silent.
local SPAN_M = 100

-- Mark spacing, as a multiple of the mark size. Marks want a clear gap between them or the
-- trail reads as a smear; 1.4 leaves roughly a third of a mark of space. Derived from the
-- size rather than configured separately so the spacing stays right when the size changes.
local STEP_FACTOR = 1.4

-- Metres from the target below which the trail is not drawn at all.
--
-- Its own gate, well clear of iqm_nav's ARRIVE_D of 4 m, and the two are right to differ. A
-- ground route at 6 m is still a line you walk along; a minimap trail at 6 m is three marks
-- in a smudge under the player arrow, telling you nothing you cannot already see, on the
-- one part of the HUD where space is scarcest.
local NEAR_HIDE = 10.0

-- How far the player must WALK, in metres, before the scale can be solved. Below this the
-- denominator is small enough that subpixel measurement error starts to matter. No direction
-- is required -- map-local space carries no rotation, so any four metres will do.
local MOVE_SEP = 4.0

-- Sanity band for a solved scale, in UI px per metre. The measured value on this install
-- is ~0.85 (section 8, F3e); a map twice as tight or four times as wide would both be
-- surprising, and either means we solved off a spot that was not where we thought.
local PPM_LO, PPM_HI = 0.2, 8.0

-- A freshly-added spot has no position for one frame -- CMapLocation builds the widget at
-- construction but CUIMiniMap::UpdateSpots is what places it, so GetAbsoluteRect reports
-- the unplaced rect sitting at the UI origin (section 4.2). Anything this close to (0,0)
-- is that, not a real measurement.
local ORIGIN_EPS = 2.0

-- The mark STYLES, keyed by the value iqm_core pushes from the menu's minimap_style list.
--
-- A table rather than a boolean branch because what the player is choosing is one thing --
-- which glyph the trail is made of -- and each style is a whole little design: the widget
-- pair it is built from and whether it carries a direction. A third style is a row here and
-- a string, rather than a second flag crossed with the first.
--
-- `headed` is not cosmetic. An arrow has to be re-headed every frame, since the map is
-- heading-up and a fixed world bearing rotates under it (section 8.1); a dot has no
-- direction to state, so it skips the SetHeading entirely.
--
-- `sh` is the dark backing copy, drawn 2 px larger behind the mark, and it is OPTIONAL. It
-- is how every other small glyph in this mod gets its outline, and at an 8 px minimap mark
-- it fails twice over: 2 px is 25% oversize at that scale, and the two widgets round to
-- screen pixels independently, so the ring reads as heavy AND visibly off centre. The dot
-- therefore carries its keyline in the texture (tools/dot-tex/build.py) and declares no
-- backing at all. The arrows keep theirs -- they are drawn larger, and a chevron's outline
-- has no fixed side to be concentric with.
local STYLES = {
	[0] = { name = "arrows", el = "route_arrow", sh = "route_arrow_sh", headed = true  },
	[1] = { name = "dots",   el = "minimap_dot", sh = nil,              headed = false },
}
local DEF_STYLE = 0

-- `argb` is the packed form of r/g/b. It is cached HERE, on the config, rather than rebuilt
-- in place(): place() runs up to MAX_MARKS times every frame and the colour changes only when
-- the config does. set_config is the single assignment site, and it is also exactly what runs
-- when an MCM slider moves -- so the pack stays as live as the sliders are.
local C = { on = false, size = 8, style = DEF_STYLE, dist = 50, r = 255, g = 255, b = 255,
            argb = GetARGB(255, 255, 255, 255) }

-- The backing's wash: a constant, built once at load. It is the mark's OUTLINE and is
-- deliberately NOT tinted with the player's colour (see place()), so nothing about it varies
-- and there is nothing to recompute per mark per frame.
local SH_ARGB = GetARGB(178, 0, 0, 0)

--- The current style, never nil: an out-of-range value from a stale MCM entry falls back to
--  the default rather than indexing nil in ensure() and taking the trail down.
local function style()
	return STYLES[C.style] or STYLES[DEF_STYLE]
end

local DEBUG = false

-- printf substitutes the literal "%s" and nothing else, so every value reaching this
-- call is pre-formatted into a string. The two lines that did the work are
-- iqm_util.logger's now -- seven modules were carrying the same pair, differing only
-- in the tag and in which flag gated them. The gate is a FUNCTION rather than a
-- value: DEBUG is flipped at run time, and capturing it once would freeze the logger
-- at whatever it was when the file loaded.
local say = iqm_util.logger("[IQM-MM] ", function() return DEBUG end)

local function f2(v) return string.format("%.2f", v) end

-- Scratch vectors, so placing a mark allocates nothing per frame -- the same arrangement
-- iqm_core uses for its own placement helpers.
local _p, _s = vector2(), vector2()
-- ...and one scratch Frect for the same reason. GetAbsoluteRect FILLS whatever it is handed
-- and the four edges are read out of it on the very next line, so the Frect actor_read used
-- to build per frame was pure garbage -- allocated, overwritten, read once, dropped.
local _r = Frect()
local function P(x, y) return _p:set(x, y) end
local function S(x, y) return _s:set(x, y) end

-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Calibration state
-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- CAL is the solved transform, valid for one level at one resolution. It is CACHED rather
-- than re-solved per frame: the centre and scale change only on level change, resolution
-- change or HUD rescale, and re-solving every frame would need a live reference spot every
-- frame for no gain.
-- TWO scales, not one, and this is the thing that took a session to see.
--
-- Marks are placed in the 1024x768 virtual UI, but that space is not square on screen: a UI
-- delta (dx, dy) is drawn at (dx * w/1024, dy * h/768), so the two axes carry different
-- numbers of real pixels per UI unit on any display that is not 4:3. The minimap is a
-- CIRCLE on screen, which means in UI coordinates it is an ELLIPSE -- and one metre east is
-- therefore a different number of UI units than one metre north.
--
-- A single scalar px/m is wrong by a factor that depends on which way the route runs, and
-- since the map is heading-up that factor CHANGES AS THE PLAYER TURNS. The symptom is marks
-- that swing with rotation and a trail that does not leave the player toward the target.
--
-- The relationship could be computed -- it is UI_KX -- but section 4's whole argument is
-- that measuring beats modelling here, and measuring both axes costs nothing extra: the
-- rotation is already known, so each axis is its own one-dimensional solve.
--
-- `lvl`, `w` and `h` are the three things that can invalidate the transform, kept as VALUES
-- so the per-frame staleness test is three comparisons rather than a string build. `key` is
-- the same three joined up, and it stays: status() surfaces it and debug_state prints it, and
-- neither of those is a per-frame path.
local CAL = { ok = false, cx = 0, cy = 0, px = 0, py = 0, xok = false, yok = false, key = nil,
              lvl = nil, w = 0, h = 0 }

-- The previous observation of the ACTOR's own spot: where the engine put it in MAP-LOCAL
-- coordinates, and where the actor stood in the world at that moment. Differencing two of
-- these gives the map's scale directly, because map-local space is metres times the zoom
-- factor with no rotation in it (CMapLocation::UpdateSpot -> ConvertRealToLocal).
local OBS = { have = false, lx = 0, ly = 0, wx = 0, wz = 0 }

-- Why the last calibration attempt did or did not get a reference. Purely diagnostic, and
-- it exists because the first build of this module drew nothing and the only way to tell a
-- missing target from a missing SPOT TYPE was to guess. `IQM: Minimap State` prints it.
local LAST = { id = nil, spot = nil, why = nil }

-- The object our own calibration spot is currently on, if any, so it can be taken off
-- again. Leaving one behind would put a stray pixel on someone's minimap for the rest of
-- the session, which is exactly the kind of litter §7 says not to ship.
local OWN = { id = nil }

--- Put our calibration spot on an object, taking it off whatever had it before.
--
--  Guarded on iqm_calib_declared, which modxml_n_iqm_map_icons.script sets only once it has
--  actually spliced the type in. Asking the engine for an unknown spot type is not a soft
--  failure -- CMapLocation::Load asserts on the missing node -- so on an install without
--  DXML support this never fires and the trail simply does not calibrate.
--- Did the DXML pass actually declare our spot type?
--
--  Read through the owning script's NAMESPACE rather than as a plain global. A .script runs
--  in its own environment table, so the `iqm_calib_declared = true` in
--  modxml_n_iqm_map_icons.script sets a field on that module and never a global -- reading
--  it bare here returns nil forever, which is precisely how the first version of this
--  failed: DXML ran, logged that it had patched, and the flag still read false.
local function calib_declared()
	local m = modxml_n_iqm_map_icons
	return (m and m.iqm_calib_declared) and true or false
end

local function own_spot(id)
	if not (calib_declared() and level.map_add_object_spot) then return false end
	if OWN.id == id then return true end
	if OWN.id then
		if not level.map_has_object_spot or level.map_has_object_spot(OWN.id, CALIB) ~= 0 then
			level.map_remove_object_spot(OWN.id, CALIB)
		end
	end
	OWN.id = nil
	if not id then return false end
	if level.map_has_object_spot and level.map_has_object_spot(id, CALIB) ~= 0 then
		OWN.id = id
		return true
	end
	level.map_add_object_spot(id, CALIB, "")
	OWN.id = id
	return true
end

local function drop_own()
	if OWN.id then own_spot(nil) end
end

--- Throw the whole transform away, both axes included.
--
--  A helper rather than three assignments at each call site, because forgetting one of the
--  per-axis flags leaves a HALF-cleared transform: `ok` goes false, the axis flags stay
--  true, and the next calibration pass then "completes" instantly using scales measured
--  before whatever invalidated them.
local function cal_clear()
	CAL.ok, CAL.xok, CAL.yok = false, false, false
	-- The scales go too, not just the flags. Leaving them readable means a diagnostic can
	-- print a stale scale next to "not calibrated" and read as though one were live.
	CAL.s, CAL.px, CAL.py = 0, 0, 0
	OBS.have = false
end

--- The three things that can move the transform under us, as plain values: which level we
--  are on, and the screen's two dimensions.
--
--  All three are compared, and dropping any one of them reintroduces the half-stale transform
--  cal_clear's comment is about -- a level change with the resolution unchanged, or a window
--  resize that keeps one axis, would both go unnoticed.
local function cal_now()
	local dev = device()
	return (level and level.name and level.name() or "?"),
	       (dev and dev.width or 0), (dev and dev.height or 0)
end

--- The same three as one printable key, for status() and the F7 dump.
--
--  Built only when the transform is (re)solved, never per frame. It used to be rebuilt every
--  frame purely to compare against itself: a device() call, a level.name() call, two tostrings
--  and three concatenations -- and an interned string -- to detect two events that essentially
--  never happen. The components above answer that in three comparisons.
local function cal_key()
	local lvl, w, h = cal_now()
	return lvl .. "|" .. tostring(w) .. "x" .. tostring(h)
end

--- Read the ACTOR's own calibration spot: where the engine DREW it (absolute UI units) and
--  where it PUT it (map-local units). Returns cx, cy, lx, ly, or nil.
--
--  THE ACTOR, and nothing else, and this is the whole point. Every previous version needed a
--  reference object near the player -- first the route target, then any carded NPC, then any
--  online object -- and all of them shared the same defect: when nothing was close enough,
--  the trail never calibrated and so never appeared. A navigation aid that requires you to
--  already be next to something is not much of a navigation aid.
--
--  Two engine facts make the actor sufficient on its own:
--
--   1. The actor sits at the map centre by construction (UpdateRadar centres the map on the
--      camera), so its spot is always inside the visible rect, always attached, and its
--      ABSOLUTE position is the centre we need -- measured live, every frame, with no
--      movement required at all.
--
--   2. CMapLocation::UpdateSpot calls SetWndPos UNCONDITIONALLY and only gates AttachChild
--      on IsRectVisible (map_location.cpp:396-414). So a spot's MAP-LOCAL position is always
--      current, and map-local space is world metres times the map's zoom factor with no
--      rotation in it (ConvertRealToLocal). Walk a few metres and the actor's own local
--      position moves by exactly that scale.
--
--  So the centre comes from the absolute rect and the scale from the local one, both off a
--  spot that is always there. The earlier F3c "freeze" was never the engine refusing to
--  update the spot -- it was this module reading GetAbsoluteRect on a spot that had been
--  DETACHED from the map, which is a different failure with the same appearance.
local function actor_read(act)
	LAST.spot, LAST.why = nil, nil
	local id = act:id()
	LAST.id = id

	if not own_spot(id) then
		LAST.why = "iqm_calib was never declared -- no DXML support?"
		return nil
	end
	local st = level.map_get_object_minimap_spot_static
	           and level.map_get_object_minimap_spot_static(id, CALIB)
	if not st then
		LAST.why = "the actor's calibration spot has no widget yet"
		return nil
	end
	LAST.spot = CALIB

	local r = _r
	st:GetAbsoluteRect(r)
	local cx, cy = (r.x1 + r.x2) * 0.5, (r.y1 + r.y2) * 0.5
	-- section 4.2: a widget added this frame exists but has not been placed, and reports at
	-- the UI origin. One frame later it is real.
	if cx > -ORIGIN_EPS and cx < ORIGIN_EPS and cy > -ORIGIN_EPS and cy < ORIGIN_EPS then
		LAST.why = "waiting for the calibration spot to be placed"
		return nil
	end

	-- The MAP-LOCAL half is only ever wanted while the scale is still being solved. Once it
	-- is, calibrate() takes the centre above and returns before it looks at lx/ly, so reading
	-- them was an allocation (GetWndPos builds a vector2) for a value nothing went on to use.
	-- The centre itself is still re-read every frame -- see calibrate().
	--
	-- This makes the return ARITY depend on CAL.ok, which is why calibrate's guard is keyed on
	-- `cx` and not on the local coordinates.
	if CAL.ok then return cx, cy end

	if not st.GetWndPos then
		LAST.why = "GetWndPos is not bound on this build"
		return nil
	end
	local lp = st:GetWndPos()
	if not lp then
		LAST.why = "GetWndPos returned nothing"
		return nil
	end
	return cx, cy, lp.x, lp.y
end

--- World XZ offset -> UI orientation, in metres. Rotation only; scale is applied later.
--
--  Screen y grows DOWNWARD while world z grows north, hence the negated z. The map is
--  heading-up with rot = -camera heading (section 8.1), so this is the map's own rotation
--  reproduced exactly rather than inferred.
local function orient(wx, wz, c, s)
	return wx * c + wz * s, wx * s - wz * c
end

--- Camera heading terms for this frame: cos and sin of the map's rotation.
local function cam_rot()
	local cam = device().cam_dir
	local rot = -atan2(cam.x, cam.z)
	return cos(rot), sin(rot)
end

--- Read the actor's spot and, once the player has walked far enough, solve.
--
--  The CENTRE is taken every frame, not solved: the actor's spot IS the centre, so there is
--  nothing to derive and nothing to go stale. It also self-corrects when the player changes
--  HUD scale or resolution mid-session.
--
--  The SCALE comes from the map-local position, which moves as the player walks:
--
--      |local(t2) - local(t1)| / |world(t2) - world(t1)|   =  UI units per metre
--
--  Map-local space carries no rotation, so this is a plain ratio of lengths with no
--  trigonometry and nothing to get the sign of wrong. It needs MOVE_SEP metres of walking
--  and no particular direction -- which every player does within seconds of loading.
--
--  The two AXIS scales then differ only by the aspect correction the engine applies when it
--  rotates the map: the map is drawn circular on screen, so in the 1024x768 virtual UI --
--  which is not square on screen -- its x is scaled by UI_KX relative to its y. That factor
--  is pure display arithmetic, knowable exactly from the resolution, and is the one part of
--  this that is computed rather than measured. Section 4's "measure, do not model" argument
--  is about the map's rect and zoom, which vary by mod and load order; UI_KX does not.
local function calibrate(act)
	local cx, cy, lx, ly = actor_read(act)
	if not cx then return end

	-- Live centre, every frame, whether or not the scale is known yet.
	CAL.cx, CAL.cy = cx, cy

	-- The scale is a property of the level and the layout, so it is solved once and kept.
	-- The centre above is not: it is re-read every frame, which is what makes the transform
	-- survive a HUD rescale without anyone noticing.
	if CAL.ok then return end

	local ap = act:position()
	if not OBS.have then
		OBS.lx, OBS.ly, OBS.wx, OBS.wz, OBS.have = lx, ly, ap.x, ap.z, true
		return
	end

	local dwx, dwz = ap.x - OBS.wx, ap.z - OBS.wz
	local dw = sqrt(dwx * dwx + dwz * dwz)
	if dw < MOVE_SEP then return end

	local dlx, dly = lx - OBS.lx, ly - OBS.ly
	local dl = sqrt(dlx * dlx + dly * dly)
	local s = dl / dw

	if s < PPM_LO or s > PPM_HI then
		say("rejected scale %s px/m from %s m of walking -- re-anchoring", f2(s), f2(dw))
		OBS.have = false
		return
	end

	CAL.s = s
	CAL.px, CAL.py = s * UI_KX, s
	CAL.xok, CAL.yok = true, true
	-- The three components AND the printable key, together: the components are what the
	-- per-frame staleness test compares, the key is what the diagnostics print.
	CAL.lvl, CAL.w, CAL.h = cal_now()
	CAL.ok, CAL.key = true, cal_key()
	say("calibrated off the actor: centre %s,%s  local scale %s px/m over %s m  "
	    .. "-> %s x / %s y (UI_KX %s)",
		f2(CAL.cx), f2(CAL.cy), f2(s), f2(dw), f2(CAL.px), f2(CAL.py), f2(UI_KX))
end

-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The overlay window
-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Its own dialog rather than another layer inside IqmCards: the card window is already at
-- 180/198 locals' worth of module around it, this needs none of its state, and a separate
-- AddDialogToRender lands in the same DoRenderDialogs pass (section 2) so it draws over the
-- minimap exactly the same way.
class "IqmMiniMap" (CUIScriptWnd)

function IqmMiniMap:__init() super()
	self:SetWndRect(Frect():set(0, 0, UI_W, UI_H))
	self.xml = CScriptXmlInit()
	self.xml:ParseFile("iqm_cards.xml")
	-- Widgets are built lazily, on the first frame that actually wants one. A player who
	-- never has a route -- or who turns this off -- pays for nothing.
	self.marks = {}
	self.built = 0
	-- How many marks are CURRENTLY visible. `built` is a high-water mark and never shrinks;
	-- this is the live count, and hide_from depends on the difference between the two.
	self.shown_n = 0
end

function IqmMiniMap:__finalize() end

--- Ensure `n` mark widgets exist, in the current style.
--
--  One widget, or two: a style that names an `sh` gets a dark backing copy under the mark,
--  which is what keeps a small sprite legible over map terrain of any brightness -- the same
--  two-widget idiom as the AR route's chevrons. A style whose glyph carries its own keyline
--  gets one widget and no backing at all; see STYLES.
function IqmMiniMap:ensure(n)
	-- Called once per placed mark, so up to MAX_MARKS times a frame, and after the first frame
	-- at a given trail length there is nothing for it to do. Answer that case before touching
	-- style() or the loop. The MAX_MARKS clamp below still applies to the build itself.
	if n <= self.built then return end
	if n > MAX_MARKS then n = MAX_MARKS end
	local sty = style()
	while self.built < n do
		local i = self.built + 1
		-- Every widget these name already exists in iqm_cards.xml over textures already
		-- declared in iqm_textures.xml. The style is read once per call rather than per
		-- widget: configure() dumps the pool on any change, so every widget in it was built
		-- from the same style by construction.
		local sh = sty.sh and self.xml:InitStatic(sty.sh, self) or nil
		local s  = self.xml:InitStatic(sty.el, self)
		-- Heading is ENABLED once, here, and never again: it is a property of the widget and
		-- of the style it was built from, and configure() dumps the pool on any style change,
		-- so a widget can never outlive the style that decided this. Only SetHeading stays per
		-- frame, and it has to -- the map is heading-up, so a fixed world bearing rotates
		-- under it every time the player turns.
		if sty.headed then
			if sh then sh:EnableHeading(true) end
			s:EnableHeading(true)
		end
		if sh then sh:Show(false) end
		s:Show(false)
		self.marks[i] = { sh = sh, s = s }
		self.built = i
	end
end

--- Hide every mark above `n`.
--
--  Up to the last SHOWN count, not up to the pool's high-water mark. `built` never shrinks, so
--  walking to it meant the idle case -- hide_from(0), which is what runs on every frame with
--  no route, no calibration, a near target or the trail switched off -- issued up to MAX_MARKS
--  engine Show(false) calls per frame on widgets that were already hidden, for the rest of the
--  session, once a long trail had been drawn even once.
--
--  The invariant: place() is the ONLY site that ever calls Show(true), ensure() builds hidden,
--  and Refresh always follows its run of place() calls with a hide_from. So marks 1..shown_n
--  are exactly the visible ones and everything above is known to be hidden already. The pool
--  dump in set_config resets shown_n alongside built, since a counter pointing into a pool
--  that no longer exists would both strand marks visible and index a nil.
function IqmMiniMap:hide_from(n)
	for i = n + 1, self.shown_n do
		local m = self.marks[i]
		if m.sh then m.sh:Show(false) end
		m.s:Show(false)
	end
	self.shown_n = n
end

--- Place one mark. `ang` is nil for a bead, a screen-space heading for an arrow.
--
--  No alpha parameter: marks are opaque and the backing, where there is one, is a fixed dark
--  wash behind them. Every attempt at varying the alpha here has encoded something that
--  belonged to the AR view rather than to this one -- see the note in Refresh.
--
--  A style with no `sh` (STYLES) places one widget. Its outline is in its texture, which is
--  the only way to keep a keyline concentric at this size: two widgets are positioned
--  independently and round to screen pixels independently.
function IqmMiniMap:place(i, ux, uy, size, ang)
	local m = self.marks[i]
	if m.sh then
		local hs = size + 2
		m.sh:SetWndSize(S(hs, hs))
		m.sh:SetWndPos(P(ux - hs * 0.5, uy - hs * 0.5))
	end
	m.s:SetWndSize(S(size, size))
	m.s:SetWndPos(P(ux - size * 0.5, uy - size * 0.5))
	if ang then
		-- A square rotated sprite takes no UI_KX squeeze of its own; the heading was
		-- already computed in screen space. Same rule as the AR route's chevrons.
		--
		-- EnableHeading is NOT re-set here. It is enabled once in ensure(), when the widget is
		-- built from a style that carries a direction; re-asserting it every frame on an
		-- already-heading-enabled widget was MAX_MARKS engine calls a frame saying nothing.
		if m.sh then m.sh:SetHeading(ang) end
		m.s:SetHeading(ang)
	end
	-- Alpha stays 255: the marks are opaque, and the three things that varied it here
	-- were all wrong (see the note above and section 6f). The COLOUR is the player's,
	-- and it multiplies the art -- so the keyline baked into both glyphs stays black
	-- whatever is set, because black times anything is black.
	-- Both colours are pre-packed: C.argb by set_config, SH_ARGB once at load. This runs up
	-- to MAX_MARKS times a frame and neither value can have changed since.
	m.s:SetTextureColor(C.argb)
	m.s:Show(true)
	if m.sh then
		-- ...and the backing is NOT tinted with it. It is the mark's outline, not part
		-- of the mark: colouring it would turn a dark rim into a second coloured sprite
		-- 2 px larger, which is a halo rather than a keyline.
		m.sh:SetTextureColor(SH_ARGB)
		m.sh:Show(true)
	end
end

--- One frame: resample the route into evenly spaced marks and place them.
--
--  NOT the published chevrons. `cp[]` is spaced for the AR view -- 3 m by default -- and at
--  the measured ~0.85 px/m that is 2.5 px between 8 px sprites, so a chevron trail piles
--  into a smear rather than reading as a line. Section 5 said as much ("that same list at a
--  DIFFERENT spacing") and the first build ignored it.
--
--  So the stroke vertices `p[]` are walked as a polyline and a mark is emitted every
--  STEP_FACTOR x mark-size of SCREEN distance. Spacing is then a property of how the trail
--  looks rather than of how far the ground chevrons happen to be apart, and it stays right
--  when the player changes the mark size or the map scale changes under them.
--
--  The walk starts AT THE ACTOR rather than at the head of the published stroke. That was
--  item 2 back when iqm_nav trimmed NEAR_TRIM = 3 m off the near end for first-person
--  reasons that mean nothing on a top-down map; R2.25 has since taken that trim to zero for
--  the ground line too, so the two views now agree on where a route begins. This still runs
--  from the actor rather than from p[1] -- it costs nothing, and it is the map's own
--  guarantee that the trail leaves the player arrow whatever the ground line does next.
function IqmMiniMap:Refresh(act)
	local rd = iqm_nav and iqm_nav.route_draw and iqm_nav.route_draw()
	if not (rd and rd.n > 0 and CAL.ok) then
		self:hide_from(0)
		return
	end
	-- Where the target is: metres to it, and its x and z. iqm_nav has already computed all
	-- three, so this is three table reads rather than a second target resolution.
	--
	-- NOT status(), which this used to call once a frame for exactly these three fields.
	-- status() builds a fresh ~20-entry hash every call and measures the snap distance on the
	-- way through, which is right for the F7 dump and wasted work here.
	--
	-- Called as a STATEMENT, and never as `local d, x, z = f and f()`. `and` is an expression
	-- and an expression TRUNCATES a multiple return to one value -- a trap this codebase has
	-- already been caught by once, and one that fails quietly: the distance would survive and
	-- the goal would vanish, so the trail would simply stop marking the target.
	local tdist, tgx, tgz
	if iqm_nav.route_target then tdist, tgx, tgz = iqm_nav.route_target() end

	-- Close enough that the trail says nothing, tested BEFORE any of the work below. This used
	-- to sit after route_limit's scan, so inside NEAR_HIDE the drawing stopped and the work did
	-- not. The two reads are independent, so the early-out belongs first.
	--
	-- `tdist` is nil until a target first resolves, and nil again once there is no objective.
	-- That is deliberately NOT the near case: an unknown distance draws whatever route is
	-- published rather than hiding.
	if tdist and tdist < NEAR_HIDE then
		self:hide_from(0)
		return
	end

	-- The list runs as far as the LONGER of the two views wants (R2.24), so take our own
	-- share of it. `rn` stands in for rd.n everywhere below; the walk only ever reads
	-- vertices, so the chevron count route_limit also returns is of no use here.
	local rn = rd.n
	if iqm_nav.route_limit then rn = iqm_nav.route_limit(C.dist) end

	local c, s = cam_rot()
	local ap = act:position()
	local headed = style().headed

	-- The whole walk happens in WORLD METRES, and only the final placement converts to UI.
	-- That is what makes spacing and culling rotation-independent: rotation preserves
	-- length, so a distance in the rotated frame IS a distance in metres, whereas a
	-- distance in UI units depends on which way the route happens to run (see CAL).
	-- Two bounds on how far the trail may run, and the smaller wins.
	--
	--   SPAN_M is the engine reading (section 3): 100 m across wherever minimap_zoom is
	--   unset. It is an assumption, and if it is too generous marks land outside the map's
	--   circle -- visible litter on the HUD.
	--
	--   The SCREEN EDGE is a fact. The map is centred at the measured (cx, cy) and cannot
	--   extend past the display, so its radius is at most the distance from that centre to
	--   the nearest edge of the 1024x768 UI. Converted through the measured scales, that is
	--   a hard ceiling in metres which needs no assumption at all -- and on a minimap tucked
	--   into a corner it is much tighter than SPAN_M.
	local edge_x = (CAL.cx < UI_W - CAL.cx) and CAL.cx or (UI_W - CAL.cx)
	local edge_y = (CAL.cy < UI_H - CAL.cy) and CAL.cy or (UI_H - CAL.cy)
	local rad_m = SPAN_M * 0.5
	if CAL.px > 0 and edge_x / CAL.px < rad_m then rad_m = edge_x / CAL.px end
	if CAL.py > 0 and edge_y / CAL.py < rad_m then rad_m = edge_y / CAL.py end
	-- Room for the sprite itself, and nothing more.
	rad_m = rad_m - (C.size * EDGE_MARKS) / CAL.py
	if rad_m < 1 then rad_m = 1 end
	-- Spacing is asked for in UI units and converted through the y scale, which is the axis
	-- the virtual UI does not stretch.
	local step_m = (C.size * STEP_FACTOR) / CAL.py
	if step_m < 0.25 then step_m = 0.25 end

	-- Previous vertex as a world offset from the actor. Starts at the actor itself, so the
	-- first segment runs from the player to the head of the stroke.
	local ox, oz = 0, 0
	-- Metres since the last emitted mark. Seeded at step_m so the first mark lands at
	-- offset zero, i.e. on the player.
	local carry = step_m
	local n = 0
	local last_ang = nil

	-- The DESTINATION as one extra vertex past the end of the stroke, when we know where it
	-- is. The stroke stops at draw_m, so its last point is the target only when the route is
	-- short enough to reach it -- and a trail that stops somewhere near the objective
	-- without marking it makes the reader guess which of the last few marks meant "here".
	--
	-- Appending it also closes the gap the other way: if the stroke already ends at the
	-- target the extra vertex is a zero-length segment, which the seg > 0.0001 test drops.
	local gx, gz = nil, nil
	if tgx and tgz then gx, gz = tgx - ap.x, tgz - ap.z end
	local last_i = rn + (gx and 1 or 0)

	for i = 1, last_i do
		local qx, qz
		if i <= rn then
			local p = rd.p[i]
			qx, qz = p.x - ap.x, p.z - ap.z
		else
			qx, qz = gx, gz
		end
		local dx, dz = qx - ox, qz - oz
		local seg = sqrt(dx * dx + dz * dz)

		if seg > 0.0001 then
			local ux, uz = dx / seg, dz / seg

			-- Heading once per segment rather than once per mark, taken from the ROTATED
			-- direction. The map is circular on screen, so the rotated world direction is
			-- the screen direction -- which is what SetHeading wants, and it needs no UI_KX
			-- correction of its own because the two axis scales already carry it.
			local ang = nil
			if headed then
				local edx, edy = orient(ux, uz, c, s)
				local el = sqrt(edx * edx + edy * edy)
				if el < 0.0001 then edx, edy, el = 0, 1, 1 end
				ang = atan2(-edy / el, edx / el)
			end
			last_ang = ang

			local t = step_m - carry
			while t <= seg do
				local wx, wz = ox + ux * t, oz + uz * t
				local rr = sqrt(wx * wx + wz * wz)          -- metres from the player
				-- Outside the map is simply dropped. A mark is either on the map or it is
				-- not; there is no partial mark to trim, which is why this renderer needs
				-- no clipping (section 5). The walk continues, since a route that leaves
				-- the map can curve back into it.
				if rr <= rad_m then
					n = n + 1
					if n > MAX_MARKS then self:hide_from(MAX_MARKS); return end
					self:ensure(n)

					-- FULLY OPAQUE, deliberately, and this is the second time the alpha
					-- here has been wrong for the view.
					--
					-- It first borrowed iqm_nav's a[], which is a camera occlusion test --
					-- with route_xray off an occluded point eases to ZERO, so in a built-up
					-- area the whole trail vanished. That was replaced by a ramp toward the
					-- map rim, which was defensible but solved a problem the cull already
					-- solves: marks are dropped before they reach the edge, so the ramp was
					-- dimming perfectly readable marks in the middle of the map for the
					-- sake of a boundary they never touch.
					--
					-- What remains is the dark backing behind each mark, which is not
					-- fading -- it is what keeps a white chevron legible over pale terrain.
					local ex, ey = orient(wx, wz, c, s)
					self:place(n, CAL.cx + CAL.px * ex, CAL.cy + CAL.py * ey, C.size, ang)
				end
				t = t + step_m
			end

			carry = (carry + seg) % step_m
			ox, oz = qx, qz
		end
	end

	-- A mark ON THE END, always. The walk emits at whole multiples of step_m, so it stops at
	-- the last one that fits and leaves up to a full step of unmarked trail -- which is the
	-- gap you see between the last mark and either the map's edge or the objective. Since
	-- the end vertex is the destination whenever we know it, this is also what puts a mark
	-- on the target itself.
	--
	-- Skipped when a mark has just been placed near there anyway, so the tail does not
	-- double up into a blob.
	if n > 0 and n < MAX_MARKS and carry >= step_m * 0.2 then
		local rr = sqrt(ox * ox + oz * oz)
		if rr <= rad_m then
			n = n + 1
			self:ensure(n)
			local ex, ey = orient(ox, oz, c, s)
			self:place(n, CAL.cx + CAL.px * ex, CAL.cy + CAL.py * ey, C.size, last_ang)
		end
	end

	self:hide_from(n)
end

-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Wiring
-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
local WND   = nil
local shown = false

--- Build the window and attach it to the HUD, lazily: get_hud() is nil at on_game_start
--  because the HUD is not built yet. Same lifecycle as iqm_core' ensure_cards.
local function ensure_wnd()
	if WND then return true end
	local hud = get_hud()
	if not hud then return false end
	WND = IqmMiniMap()
	hud:AddDialogToRender(WND)
	return true
end

local function actor_on_update()
	if not C.on then
		if shown and WND then WND:hide_from(0); shown = false end
		drop_own()
		return
	end
	local act = db.actor
	if not act then return end
	if not ensure_wnd() then return end

	-- UI_KX only changes with the screen; recompute on change, not per frame.
	local dev = device()
	if dev and dev.width and dev.width > 0 and dev.width ~= _ui_w then
		_ui_w = dev.width
		UI_KX = (dev.height / dev.width) / (768 / 1024)
	end

	-- Drop a stale transform: a level change moves the map's bounds and a resolution change
	-- moves everything. Either invalidates both the centre and the scale.
	--
	-- Compared as three VALUES rather than through cal_key(): this runs every frame to catch
	-- two events that essentially never happen, and the string the key builds was allocated
	-- and interned each time purely to be thrown away. `dev` is the one already read above.
	if CAL.ok then
		local lvl = level and level.name and level.name() or "?"
		if lvl ~= CAL.lvl
		   or (dev and dev.width or 0) ~= CAL.w
		   or (dev and dev.height or 0) ~= CAL.h then
			cal_clear()
			-- A level change unloads the object our spot was on, so forget it rather than
			-- trying to remove a spot from an id that no longer means anything.
			OWN.id = nil
			say("transform dropped -- level or resolution changed")
		end
	end
	-- Every frame, not just while uncalibrated: the centre is re-read from the actor's spot
	-- each time, and calibrate() returns immediately once the scale is known.
	calibrate(act)

	WND:Refresh(act)
	shown = true
end

--- The one assignment site for this module's config. Both entry points below land here
--  -- apply_config with what it pulled, configure with what a test driver handed it --
--  so the defaults and the pool dump can never apply to one path and not the other.
local function set_config(o_on, o_size, o_style, o_dist, o_r, o_g, o_b, o_debug)
	C.on    = o_on and true or false
	C.size  = o_size or C.size
	-- The trail's own drawn distance (R2.24). iqm_nav publishes at the longer of this
	-- and the ground line's, so what arrives here is how much of that list is ours.
	C.dist  = o_dist or C.dist
	-- An unknown style is kept as given rather than clamped here, so status() reports what
	-- was actually asked for; style() is what falls back to the default when it draws.
	C.style = o_style or DEF_STYLE
	-- The tint, applied per frame in place(), so unlike the style it needs no pool dump
	-- and takes effect on the very next frame -- which is what makes dragging the three
	-- sliders in MCM show you the trail changing colour as you drag.
	C.r     = o_r or 255
	C.g     = o_g or 255
	C.b     = o_b or 255
	-- Packed once, here, rather than per mark per frame in place(). This is the site an MCM
	-- slider move runs through, so live-dragging the three sliders still shows the trail
	-- changing colour as they move.
	C.argb  = GetARGB(255, C.r, C.g, C.b)
	DEBUG   = o_debug and true or false
	-- Style changes swap which widget a mark is built from, so the pool has to go.
	if WND then
		WND:hide_from(0)
		-- shown_n goes with built. hide_from only ever walks as far as the last shown count,
		-- so a counter left behind here would point into a pool that no longer exists: it
		-- would index a nil mark on the next short trail, and until it did it would leave the
		-- new pool's marks stranded visible.
		WND.marks, WND.built, WND.shown_n = {}, 0, 0
	end
end

--- Re-read the live config. Called by iqm_core.read_config as its last act, along
--  with every other module's apply_config -- config is PULLED here, never pushed (see
--  the seam note at the top of iqm_core). iqm_core owns the MCM reading; nothing
--  here reads an MCM option directly.
--
--  Bound inside the function, never at file scope, for the reason the seam note gives.
--
--  A second CONSUMER of iqm_nav's published route, and deliberately INDEPENDENT of the
--  AR one: `mark_route` draws the line on the ground, this draws marks on the minimap,
--  and either can be on without the other. iqm_nav's own gate paths for whichever is
--  asking. (F3, docs/minimap-route.md.)
--
--  It does NOT ride game.world2ui_with_depth either, unlike the ground line. The trail
--  never projects anything -- it measures its own transform off the minimap -- so that
--  binding is irrelevant to it.
function apply_config()
	local C2 = iqm_core.config()
	set_config((C2.mark_targets and C2.mark_minimap) and true or false,
	           C2.minimap_size, C2.minimap_style, C2.minimap_dist,
	           C2.minimap_r, C2.minimap_g, C2.minimap_b, C2.debug_log)
end

--- The same assignment, in the shape the TEST DRIVERS use (tools/minimap-harness).
--  Kept because a harness has no iqm_core to pull from, and because stating the
--  values outright is what lets a test vary one of them.
function configure(o)
	if type(o) ~= "table" then return end
	set_config(o.on, o.size, o.style, o.dist, o.r, o.g, o.b, o.debug)
end

--- What the transform currently is. For the F7 action below and for tests.
function status()
	return { ok = CAL.ok, cx = CAL.cx, cy = CAL.cy, px = CAL.px, py = CAL.py,
	         s = CAL.s, xok = CAL.xok, yok = CAL.yok, key = CAL.key,
	         style = C.style, style_name = style().name }
end

-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- F7 debug
-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
function debug_state()
	local st = status()
	printf("-IQM-MM: enabled %s   style %s (%s)   spot on the actor %s   last problem %s",
		tostring(C.on), tostring(C.style), style().name,
		tostring(OWN.id), tostring(LAST.why or "none"))
	printf("-IQM-MM: iqm_calib declared by DXML: %s", tostring(calib_declared()))

	if not st.ok then
		printf("-IQM-MM: not calibrated yet -- needs %s m of walking. No nearby object is "
		       .. "required; the actor's own spot is the reference", f2(MOVE_SEP))
		return
	end
	printf("-IQM-MM: centre %s,%s   scale %s x / %s y px/m   key %s",
		f2(st.cx), f2(st.cy), f2(st.px), f2(st.py), tostring(st.key))

	-- The two axis scales should differ by exactly the virtual UI's aspect correction. They
	-- are MEASURED rather than derived from it (section 4), so printing both alongside what
	-- UI_KX predicts turns "the marks swing when I turn" into one readable line.
	printf("-IQM-MM: measured x/y ratio %s   UI_KX predicts %s   (a mismatch here is why "
	       .. "marks would move with camera heading)",
		f2(st.py ~= 0 and (st.px / st.py) or 0), f2(UI_KX))

	-- If the trail stops short of the map edge, or runs past it, this is the line to check.
	-- SPAN_M is an engine reading rather than a measurement; the screen-edge figure beside
	-- it is a hard fact, and whichever is smaller is the one actually being used.
	local ex = (st.cx < UI_W - st.cx) and st.cx or (UI_W - st.cx)
	local ey = (st.cy < UI_H - st.cy) and st.cy or (UI_H - st.cy)
	printf("-IQM-MM: cull %s m from span, %s m / %s m from the screen edge -- smallest wins",
		f2(SPAN_M * 0.5), f2(st.px > 0 and ex / st.px or 0), f2(st.py > 0 and ey / st.py or 0))
	printf("-IQM-MM: local scale %s px/m; mark step %s m",
		f2(st.s or 0), f2(st.py ~= 0 and ((C.size * STEP_FACTOR) / st.py) or 0))
end

function debug_recalibrate()
	cal_clear()
	printf("-IQM-MM: transform cleared, will re-solve")
end

-- The once-per-process flag and the launcher guard live in iqm_util.inject_actions;
-- the list is the only part that was ever this module's own. Still deferred to
-- actor_on_first_update rather than run from on_game_start: the debugger's own
-- content table is not necessarily built when on_game_start fires.
local function inject_f7_actions()
	iqm_util.inject_actions("iqm_minimap", {
		{ name = "IQM: Minimap State",       functor = { debug_state },       hide_ui = 2 },
		{ name = "IQM: Minimap Recalibrate", functor = { debug_recalibrate }, hide_ui = 2 },
	})
end

function on_game_start()
	RegisterScriptCallback("actor_on_first_update", inject_f7_actions)
	RegisterScriptCallback("actor_on_update", actor_on_update)
end
